  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of light to moderate beneficial rain will slowly \ndrift south through Wednesday with cloudy/damp/cool conditions.\n\nA quasi-stationary area of mid-level frontogenesis that has \nbeen the focus for showers overnight along the I-80 corridor \nwill move gradually south today. North of this boundary, dry air\nwill gradually pour into the area as a sprawling high builds\ninto the northeast US. Along and to the south of the\naforementioned feature, periods of light to moderate rainfall\nwill continue through much of the day. A compact wave of energy\ndrifting across the Laurel Highlands could generate an isolated\nrumble of thunder or two. Although a rogue storm could produce\nsome heavier rainfall, rates are expected to remain low enough\nto preclude flash flooding today. Ponding in low-lying or poor\ndrainage areas cannot be ruled out, but most of the rain will be\nbeneficial.\n\nTemperatures will hold steady if not drop a bit today thanks to\npersistent easterly flow and a cold air damming pattern. Highs \nthis afternoon will be some -10 to -15F below average for this \ntime of year, generally not making it out of the 50s. Overnight,\nrain will continue to shift southwest and we could even see\nrain-free conditions for much of the night. Fog/low clouds are a\ngood bet again tonight, especially along and south of the\nmoisture gradient that will set up across the Commonwealth.\nBy Wednesday morning, dewpoints in the low 40s and perhaps upper\n30s will pour into northeast PA and signal the arrival of fall\nright on time.\n\nOn Wednesday, showers and low clouds will linger across\nsouthwest PA, while locations farther north will see improving\nconditions - albeit gradually. Easterly winds will again spell\ncooler-than-normal temperatures and stubborn clouds. Wednesday\nwill be another cool day with temperatures only a few degrees\nwarmer compared to Tuesday and highs in the upper 50s to low \n60s.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending drier later in the week as a coastal \nstorm develops offshore.\n\nNortheast flow Wednesday into Thursday between 1038 mb high in\nQuebec and a developing coastal low will advect in dewpoints in\nthe 40s and perhaps even the 30s at the sfc Wed-Thu, with even\ndrier air in the 500-850 mb layer. This should bring an end to\nthe rainfall for at least a couple of days. The next feature to\nmonitor will be the coastal storm, which may meander off the New\nEngland and Mid Atlantic coast into the early part of next week\nwith this blocking high to its north. Such blocky patterns \nare notoriously difficult to predict with respect to sfc\nfeatures and precip potential/placement/timing. Depending on the\nsize of the coastal storm and how much it retrogrades, rain will\nbe possible in at least east central PA between Fri and early\nnext week, but confidence is low. Higher confidence in wind \ngusts of 15-25 mph and below normal temps through the end of the\nweek. \n\nIf the current forecast holds true, eastern PA could be\nrather cloudy (mainly high clouds) while western PA is sunny at\nleast for the first half of the weekend. The western periphery\nof the coastal storm's cirrus deck could make for some vibrant\nsunsets. Temperatures will gradually increase into the weekend,\nreturning to near normal conditions by Saturday and Sunday.",
